Backend Architecture
Version: 2026-07-20
Status: Authoritative Backend Guide

Overview
This document describes the backend architecture of WordList Writer. It merges the original backend specification with updated architecture rules and July 19 fixes. It explains how Express is structured, how API routes connect to controllers, how Supabase is integrated, and how save and load pipelines operate. It also documents the updated highlight pipeline rules, project ID rules, master list rules, and frequency list rules that affect backend behavior.

High-Level Structure
The backend is a lightweight Express server. It provides:

static file hosting

API routes for saving and loading data

Supabase integration for persistence

middleware for JSON parsing and routing

The backend does not perform text analysis. All analysis occurs in the frontend. The backend must remain simple, predictable, and free of highlight logic.

Server Entry Point
The backend entry point is:
src/server.js

Responsibilities:

initialize Express

register middleware

register API routes

serve static files

start HTTP server

Middleware Order
Middleware order is critical:

JSON body parser

API routes

static file hosting

Static hosting must come last. If placed earlier, it will shadow API routes.

API Route Structure
API routes live in:
src/routes

Each route file:

defines endpoints

imports controllers

exports router

Example route types:

/api/project/save

/api/project/load

/api/master/save

/api/master/load

Controllers
Controllers live in:
src/controllers

Responsibilities:

validate request data

call Supabase client

transform results

return JSON responses

Controllers must not contain business logic. They only coordinate operations.

